cts in terms of weak shuffle and stuffle products.\nTitle: Weak stuffle algebras\nAuthors: Cécile Mammez\nDate: Fri Mar 20 08:23:17 2020\n\nMotivated by q-shuffle products determined by Singer from q-analogues of multiple zeta values, we build in this article a generalisation of the shuffle and stuffle products in terms of weak shuffle and stuffle products. Then, we characterise weak shuffle products and give as examples the case of an alphabet of cardinality two or three. We focus on a comparison between algebraic structures respected in the classical case and in the weak case. As in the classical case, each weak shuffle product can be equipped with a dendriform structure. However, they have another behaviour towards the quadri-algebra and the Hopf algebra structure. We give some relations satisfied by weak stuffle products.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nClearly, \\(G_0\\) satisfies these properties. If \\(G_i\\) is already defined satisfying these properties for some \\(0 \\le i \\le \\sqrt{\\log n}\\), define \\(G_{i+1}\\) as follows. We have (https://people.math.ethz.ch/~sudakovb/tight-hypergraph-cycles.pdf)\nciteturn5search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.4 years ago; Crawled: 4.3 years ago; In this paper we proved that an \\(r\\)-uniform hypergraph \\(\\mathcal H\\) on \\(n\\) vertices with \\(dn^{r-1}\\) edges, where \\(d \\ge e^{c\\sqrt{\\log n}}\\), contains a tight cycle of length at most \\(O((\\log n)^2)\\). ... Finally, let us mention a related conjecture of Conlon, see [15], about the extremal number of tight cycles of given constant length.\nOur proof can be also used to show that one can find a cycle of any specific length \\(L\\), divisible by \\(r\\), such that \\(\\Omega((\\log n)^2) < L < d e^{-O(\\sqrt{\\log n})}\\). To achieve this, one needs just to follow the remark after Lemma 4.2 and its consequences.\n\nIt is very plausible that our approach works also when \\(d\\) is polylogarithmic in \